(c) RATE-EQUALITY CHOICE
bracketTopForRate (internal/services/retirement/analysis/tax_optimizer_strategies.go)
matches a bracket's Rate to the target using exact float equality (`==`),
not an epsilon — every Rate in the versioned registry is a compile-time
literal (e.g. 0.22), never a computed value, so there is no accumulated
floating-point drift to guard against.

(d) FINDING 1 FIX SUMMARY
TestInflatedBracketTopForYear_GrowsWithCalendarYear (tax_optimizer_strategies_test.go)
previously computed its "expected" year-10 value by calling
engine.NewTaxCalculator(...).GetAdjustedBrackets(...) a second time — the
same chain inflatedBracketTopForYear itself calls — so a mis-scale inside
the engine moved both sides together and stayed green (confirmed by the
mutation check in item 3 above, run against the OLD test body before this
fix; the fix's new body correctly fails under the same mutation, see item 3
above run against the NEW body). Rewrote it to assert against the literal
MFJ 2026 22% ceiling ($211,400, IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32 §4.01) run through the
compounding formula `211400 * (1+rate/100)^n` for n=0,1,10 at 1e-6 relative
tolerance; kept one engine-agreement comparison as a secondary, explicitly
non-load-bearing check. Added a new test,
TestInflatedBracketTopForYear_SingleFiler12PercentAt2026, pinning the
Single-filer 0.12 case against the literal $50,400 (2026, n=0) — this case
did not previously exist as a dedicated test (spec item 4.1.3 asked for it;
attempt 1 omitted it). Swept the other tests added in this task:
engine/taxyears_test.go's new tests (TestResolveTaxYear_2026IsStatutory,
TestResolveTaxYear_2027ProjectsFrom2026, TestLatestAndEarliestFederalTaxYear,
TestResolveTaxYear_2024Unchanged, TestResolveTaxYear_2025NoRecordProjectsWithoutError)
and analysis/tax_constants_basis_test.go's new
TestBuildTax_2026StartPlanRestsOnSeeded2026Statutory already assert against
literals (211400, 32200, 1650, 2050, 98900, 201050, 2026, 2027, "Rev. Proc.
2025-32") or structural properties (Basis, DerivedFromYear, error-ness), not
against a second call through the code path under test — no changes needed
there.
